Enhancement: Add `--migrate` option to `backup` for zip, 7z and tar archives

Until now, backing up the contents of a zip, 7z or tar archive
required extracting it to disk first.

`backup --migrate <file>` now backs up the entries of a zip, 7z or tar
archive in place, preserving the paths inside the archive. The option
can be specified multiple times, in which case the entries of all
given archives are merged into a single snapshot, with zip, 7z and tar
archives freely combinable (each archive's format is detected by its
magic bytes and, for tar, by sniffing its content). The archives are never extracted to disk: entry
contents are decompressed on the fly and streamed to the repository,
so memory usage stays bounded no matter how large the archives are.
`restore` reproduces the entries byte for byte, including
permissions and modification times. Access and change times are
also kept when the archive stores them (PAX `atime`/`ctime` records
in tar, the `0x5455` extended-timestamp extra field in zip), and
tar entries with PAX `SCHILY.xattr.*` records are backed up
together with their extended attributes. The archives are
validated before the repository is opened, and the backup always
behaves like `--force`, as the archives have no meaningful parent
snapshot.

For 7z archives, the common compression methods are supported
(LZMA, LZMA2, Zstd, Deflate, Bzip2, LZ4, Brotli, PPMD, Delta, the
BCJ filters and stored data); encrypted or unsupported entries are
skipped with a warning.

Tar archives may be plain (`.tar`), or compressed with gzip (`.tar.gz`,
`.tgz`), xz (`.tar.xz`, `.txz`), bzip2 (`.tar.bz2`, `.tbz`) or zstd
(`.tar.zst`, `.tzst`); tar entries that are unsafe (escape the archive
root, contain NUL bytes) are skipped with a warning, absolute paths are
re-rooted inside the virtual filesystem, and hard links are backed up as
copies of their target's content. A compressed file (gzip, xz, bzip2 or
zstd) that does not contain a tar archive is backed up as a single
virtual file named after the archive without the compression suffix
(e.g. `report.gz` becomes `/report`).

